1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,500
Why Cyber Insurers Now Want Offline Backups. And where archival microfilm fits

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:19,345
A cyber insurance underwriter in 2026 wants to see backups that are offline or immutable, with a documented restore test on record, not just a backup that exists.

3
00:00:19,345 --> 00:00:24,500
Archival microfilm is offline by physical construction, not by configuration.

4
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:33,326
Encrypted backups stored offline or in immutable form, such as object lock or WORM configuration. Credential isolation from production systems.

5
00:00:33,326 --> 00:00:36,609
Documented restore tests, typically within the last 90 days.

6
00:00:36,609 --> 00:00:43,177
A backup protected only by Active Directory credentials, with no offline or immutable copy, increasingly fails this bar.

7
00:00:43,177 --> 00:00:47,500
Source: Open Metal, citing EUR-Lex (NIS2) and U.S. SEC; Kiplinger; SterlingRisk

8
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:54,349
Cyber premiums fell 2.3% in 2024, the sector's first-ever decrease, and rates were still falling 2-3% in Q3 2025.

9
00:00:54,349 --> 00:00:58,334
Kiplinger's 2026 outlook forecasts gradual premium increases for cyber lines.

10
00:00:58,334 --> 00:01:03,924
A TechRadar-cited survey found nearly 70% of insurance professionals expect more claims and higher premiums.

11
00:01:03,924 --> 00:01:10,187
Average-control businesses may see 5-10% increases; higher-risk organisations may face double-digit hikes or non-renewal.

12
00:01:10,187 --> 00:01:13,500
Sources: SterlingRisk Q3 2025; Kiplinger; TechRadar-cited survey

13
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:21,987
Insurer criterion: Offline by default. Immutable cloud: No: needs correct configuration. Offline tape or disk: Only while disconnected. Archival microfilm: Yes, always.

14
00:01:21,987 --> 00:01:28,315
Insurer criterion: Configuration risk. Immutable cloud: Yes: misconfiguration defeats it. Offline tape or disk: Low. Archival microfilm: None.

15
00:01:28,315 --> 00:01:34,999
Insurer criterion: Credential dependency. Immutable cloud: Yes. Offline tape or disk: Low. Archival microfilm: None. Insurer criterion: Restore tests.

16
00:01:34,999 --> 00:01:39,500
Immutable cloud: Ongoing, scheduled. Offline tape or disk: Periodic. Archival microfilm: None needed.

17
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:46,609
Film made to ISO 18901, processed to ISO 18917 and stored to ISO 18911 carries an LE-500 rating.

18
00:01:46,609 --> 00:01:52,400
It is a longer-horizon preservation layer for records an institution cannot afford to lose.

19
00:01:52,400 --> 00:02:01,500
It does not replace the operational digital backups insurers ask about; live systems still need tested, encrypted offline or immutable backups.

20
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:04,444
Do regulations reinforce this? Yes.

21
00:02:04,444 --> 00:02:14,389
The EU's NIS2 Directive requires ten risk-management measures under Article 21, and US-listed companies must disclose material incidents within four business days under SEC rules.

22
00:02:14,389 --> 00:02:20,500
Does microfilm replace insurer-required backups? No. It sits underneath them as a separate preservation layer.

23
00:02:21,500 --> 00:02:24,718
